{{-- Marketing chrome. Layout group 'marketing'.

    A FRAGMENT, not a document. This file used to open with a doctype, and that
    one line was the root of most of this app's structural debt: process.js:311
    gates ALL layout resolution on the absence of one, and document-shell.js:64
    makes a doctype-bearing result skip shell generation entirely. A layout with
    a doctype poisons every page that extends it exactly as if each page had
    written one — which is why nothing could safely extend this file and all 23
    marketing pages hand-rolled their own document instead.

    It also declared title and description as string slots via yield. Those are
    gone: a page sets both with useSeoMeta() in a server script, which
    additionally derives the og and twitter tags from the same two strings so
    they cannot drift. The footer yield and scripts stack are gone too — no page
    ever filled them, and an unfilled slot renders '' silently, reading as
    structure while doing nothing.

    The group name matters to the router, not just to readers. deriveLayoutGroup
    (stx-router layout-metadata.js:1-6) turns 'layouts/marketing' into group
    'marketing', and 'layouts/default' into 'default'. Different groups make
    checkLayoutChange (client.js:255) do a full-document swap when crossing
    between the marketing site and the app — and client.js:568-580, the only
    code that carries stylesheet links across a navigation, runs ONLY on that
    full-document path. Same group would mean a fragment swap, which harvests
    inline styles and never links, and a marketing page reached from the app
    would arrive without marketing.css.

    Do not add an extends directive here. A nested layout stamps the BASE
    layout's name into the marker (process.js:405-421), so both shells would
    report one group and the distinction above collapses.

    THE CHROME. SiteNav and SiteFooter are included below rather than repeated
    per page, so the nav and footer are declared once for the whole group.
    SiteNav carries the two CSS-only mega-menu dropdowns (Features, Use cases):
    no JS, panels reveal on hover and on keyboard focus via :focus-within.
    SiteFooter carries the multi-column layout with the "Compare" column that
    links the alternatives index plus one page per competitor. Both scope their
    own rules in a style block at the bottom of their own file and reuse the
    design tokens from marketing.css.

    COMMENTS. Three rules, each learned by shipping the bug:

    1. Use stx comments, not HTML ones. An HTML comment survives into the
        response and is then re-read by several consumers that do not know it is
        a comment.
    2. Never spell out a tag name inside a comment. The orphan-style extraction
        pass lifts a literal style tag out of the middle of a comment, which
        separates the opening delimiter from the closing one; the comment then
        fails to match the strip pass and ships as page text.
    3. Never quote the comment delimiters inside a comment body — that ends the
        comment early and everything after it becomes page text.

    And one exception, verified against the shipped renderer: a partial that
    contains a client script never has its template comments stripped, at any
    position in the file. That is why SiteNav.stx and SiteFooter.stx document
    themselves with JS comments inside their script blocks, and why their prose
    lives here instead. --}}

@head
{{-- Marketing-only stylesheet, deliberately NOT in config/ui.ts app.head: app
    pages never linked it and define their own conflicting .btn and .panel
    rules. The shared fonts, charset, viewport and favicon DO live in app.head —
    they are genuinely site-wide, and injectConfigHeadTags dedupes by exact href
    so nothing doubles up. --}}
<link rel="stylesheet" href="/marketing.css">
{{-- No @stack('styles') here, following the same rule default.stx states: add a
    slot when a page pushes to it, not before. Nine pages used to carry an empty
    `@push('styles')` / `@endpush` pair — index and all eight compare pages —
    which made this slot read as a live extension point fed by nine files while
    it was in fact fed by none, and established the empty pair as an idiom the
    next page would copy.

    When a page does need head CSS, restore this stack AND push to it from that
    page. Do not use a bare style block: process.js:373-384 sweeps it into the
    BODY, where the router swaps it away on the next navigation. --}}
@endhead

@include('SiteNav')

{{-- The single router container (config/ui.ts router.container). Pages must not
    declare their own: the server slices the fragment between the first opening
    tag and the last closing one, so a second one makes the span ambiguous. --}}
<main>
@yield('content')
</main>

@include('SiteFooter')
